 marathon
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

distancePatterns is scanned with notes.contains() and breaks on the first
hit, but listed "marathon" before "half marathon" — and "half marathon"
contains "marathon". The .half case was unreachable.

So "Half marathon race today, 1:28:45" was recorded as a 1:28 MARATHON at
3:23/mi. That passes the 180-900 s/mi sanity check, so it became the
high-confidence race anchor: every pace zone, the Trends fitness range and
the Train histogram marker were all derived from roughly double the
athlete's actual fitness, and the app prescribed paces they cannot hold.

Reordered so substrings come after the longer patterns they appear in, and
documented the ordering constraint so it doesn't regress. Added the
hyphenated spelling while here.
