From 9774690209087f61248ac367f0edd51f6e9c2f71 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 04:02:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 13/21] Fix 2.9 + 2.10 + 2.11: date, locale and unit errors
 that misreport by a day or a zone
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

- Goal race dates were formatted with ISO8601DateFormatter, which writes
  UTC. The picked date carries a wall-clock time, so an athlete in Los
  Angeles choosing April 27 at 6pm stored "2026-04-28" — countdown, taper
  math and race lookup all a day late. Pinned to the local timezone at
  both write sites.

- Five DateFormatters with a fixed "yyyy-MM-dd" format had no POSIX
  locale, so "yyyy" resolved in the user's calendar: a device set to the
  Japanese or Buddhist calendar produced "0008-05-12" / "2569-05-12".
  Those strings went into request URLs (no stream, no weather, ever) and
  into dictionary keys callers then failed to match (mileage charts read
  empty). The codebase already does this correctly in TrainingPlanModels
  and DailyReadService. Also dropped an unused formatter in VitalManager.

- InjuryService bucketed workout_date (a TIMESTAMPTZ instant) by UTC day
  while body_mentions.mentioned_at is a bare local DATE. Every evening run
  west of Greenwich landed a day late, so mentions found no miles: AVG VOL
  showed empty and 7-day AVG LOAD was short. Now keyed by local day.

- .mile1500 was mapped to PaceCalculator's "1500m" (0.932 mi) in
  EquivalentPaces, while its displayName, distanceInMiles/Km and all six
  other consumers treat it as a mile. Every pace from a mile goal was ~7%
  fast. The enum's raw value is "1500m" for stored-data compatibility only.

- easyPaceDelta labelled the span with the number of points plotted, not
  weeks elapsed — easy runs in weeks 1 and 12 read "/ 1 WK".

- rollingFourWeekAverage returned partial windows despite its own doc
  comment and a "4-WK AVG" legend, so the first three points were 1-, 2-
  and 3-week averages.

- thisWeeksPlanned used Calendar.current (Sunday-start in a US locale)
  while the rest of the tab uses ISO Monday, so on Sundays the PLAN
  section showed next week.
