- RunningLogApp's launch task and VoiceLogView.onAppear both ran
  requestAuthorization() plus a workout fetch against HealthKitManager.shared
  at launch, with different limits (30 vs 20), and both wrote recentWorkouts.
  Whichever finished last decided what the picker held, and on first install
  two authorization requests were in flight against one HKHealthStore. The
  launch task now owns it and publishes its (larger) result; the view only
  fills a gap.

- HistoryDetailSheet built a fresh HealthKitManager() per instance, and it
  is instantiated per page inside HistoryDetailPager's LazyHStack ForEach —
  so swiping a week of entries allocated an HKHealthStore and fired an
  authorization request plus a query per page. Its isAuthorized/readState
  also diverged from the instance the app syncs with. This is the exact
  pattern already fixed in VoiceLogView.swift:36, with a comment there
  explaining why. Now uses the shared instance.

- PaceSegment.id was `UUID()` — a new identity on every access — so any
  ForEach over segments treated the whole collection as new on every render
  pass: animations restarted, selection and scroll position reset. Now
  derived from the segment's own fields, which are fixed at sync time.
